      "id": "A-RECIPE-MEAL",
      "role": "<fromMealMap>",
      "priority": 85,
      "signal": "recipe.meal",
      "mealMap": {
        "drink": "beverage",
        "dessert": "sweet"
      },
      "note": "rev2 CORRECTION. rev1 named a field `recipe.course` that DOES NOT EXIST. The real fields are `meal` (breakfast 155 / lunch 569 / dinner 367 / snack 163 / drink 133 / dessert 113) and `category` (classical 300 / everyday 563 / international 637). Only two `meal` values carry role information directly: a recipe whose meal is `drink` IS a beverage, and one whose meal is `dessert` IS a sweet. The other four name a SLOT, not a role, and mapping them here would be exactly the proxy substitution this file forbids. They are handled structurally at priority 45 instead."
    },
    {
      "id": "A-RECIPE-COMPOSED",
      "role": "<fromMealMap>",
      "priority": 45,
      "signal": "recipe.isAuthored",
      "requires": "ingredients.count >= 2 AND steps.count >= 1",
      "mealMap": {
        "breakfast": "main",
        "lunch": "main",
        "dinner": "main",
        "snack": "side"
      },
      "note": "Deliberately placed BELOW the 60-band name phrases, so it is a fallback and never an override. The claim it makes is structural, not inferential: a row with two or more ingredients and at least one preparation step is a prepared dish, therefore it is not a raw commodity and not a seasoning. Name rules still win where they fire — 'Bathua Raita' resolves to side, 'Dry Fruit Ladoo' to sweet, 'Cilantro Coriander Herb Salt' to spice — and only recipes whose names give no signal fall through here. Accuracy is gated separately in TASK-MP7 §4 G2b because this is the weakest rule in the file."
    },
